Mandate: SOFTWARE HOUSE MANDATE (user brief): Create a minimal Win32 C++20 desktop app CounterApp. One window shows an integer click counter; left-click in the client area increments it. Use WinMain, RegisterClassEx, CreateWindowEx, and a standard message loop (user32, gdi32). Layout: include/CounterApp.hpp, src/CounterApp.cpp, src/main.cpp, root CMakeLists.txt (C++20, target_include_directories for include/). PascalCase headers. No tests. Self-contained.

EXECUTIVE SYNTHESIS (internal meetings — not user-authored):
- Modality: desktop_app
- Primary language for generated artifacts: cpp
- CEO: We are delivering a lean, native Windows desktop utility that demonstrates core Win32 capabilities with a clean, modern C++20 structure, aligning with our promise of efficient, self-contained engineering artifacts.
- CPO: The architecture is tidy and standard-compliant, with clear separation of concerns between the application logic, window management, and entry point, ensuring maintainability and a proper 'cup of tea' quality codebase.
- CTO: We will implement a clean, self-contained Win32 C++20 application using standard CMake practices, ensuring strict separation of concerns between the entry point, window management, and state logic.
Architect: honor these constraints when emitting the JSON task DAG.


HUMAN-IN-THE-LOOP (Staff Mail): When you need design, UX, product, or scope clarification from the operator, call tool staff_mail_post with parameters from (your role, e.g. Architect), subject (short), body (specific questions). This queues in-app mail (Win3.1 Staff Mail window). Post milestone updates the same way so the operator can track how the build is going. Do NOT block work waiting for mail: if there is no reply (e.g. headless smoke), proceed with reasonable defaults and state your assumptions in the artifact or in staff_mail_post as a log.

ARCHITECT: Emit JSON Task DAG only (uuid, file, blocked_by, role). Exactly one Developer task owns src/main.cpp; terminal ForgeMaster node integration_build blocked by all implementation files + CMakeLists.txt. Use conventional paths only (include/*.hpp, src/*.cpp, scripts/verify_*.sh, CMakeLists.txt) with PascalCase stems — no neon_arch_* or task_* filenames. DAG_RULE_HEADER_CPP: For every include/<Stem>.hpp (or .h) interface node with uuid H, emit a matching src/<Stem>.cpp Developer node in the same DAG and set that .cpp's blocked_by to include H (the uuid string) before any looser ordering deps so the TU cannot start until the header task has Completed. Add extra blocked_by entries when a .cpp legitimately depends on other headers. Only src/main.cpp may contain int main(). DEFINITION_OF_DONE: Every implementation node must leave a syntactically valid, conventional-path artifact; Developers run host-side compile or verify commands (run_terminal_command / execute_command) before treating work as done when the stack allows. Include scripts/verify_*.sh tasks blocked by the code they exercise when the mandate implies runtime checks. ForgeMaster integration_build remains the terminal gate. COMMON ROOM: every role posts short human updates (common_room_post) and reads the transcript before large changes. DEVELOPERS: parallel implementation — no int main() except the entry task; .cpp must #include project headers. Use the exact PascalCase API names from committed include/*.hpp in .cpp and tests (no snake_case drift). write_file content must be raw source only — never persist tool-call JSON envelopes. Prioritize src/main.cpp and CMakeLists.txt before tests/ harness work. LAYERED_BUILD_ORDER (C/C++; same idea for other stacks: project manifest → public API → implementations → entry): (1) Root CMakeLists.txt early — set CXX standard, target_include_directories(<target> PRIVATE ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/include), link libs; sources must use #include "PascalCase.hpp" not #include "include/...". (2) Commit include/*.hpp contracts. (3) src/<Stem>.cpp per header. (4) program entry last once dependencies exist. MULTI_PASS_COMPLETION: use write_file/chunked_write for brand-new files; for existing files use read_file then edit_lines (or fuzzy_patch) repeatedly until the TU is complete and shadow/integration would pass — do not stop at a skeletal first draft. SOFTWARE_HOUSE_WORKFLOW: (1) Architect emits DAG; runtime bootstraps skeleton stubs for every target_file. (2) Developers flesh stubs via edit_lines/fuzzy_patch only — write_file blocked on existing paths. (3) Loop until no NEON_BODY_PENDING markers and integration_build links. (4) Executives reopen tasks on read-only loops or empty codegen — mandate not done until ForgeMaster passes. SENTINEL: orthogonal audit. FULL AUTONOMOUS MODE.

LAYERED_BUILD_ENTRY: (4) src/main.cpp with WinMain/wWinMain last once module TUs exist. 

